he-gilder-lehrman-lincoln-pr"],"hasEnglishEdition":true,"publisherId":"publisher-penguin-random-house","summary":"Winner of the Lincoln Prize A landmark Civil War history told from a fresh, deeply researched ground-level perspective. At the crux of America’s history stand two astounding events: the immediate and complete destruction of the most powerful system of slavery in the modern world, followed by a political reconstruction in which new constitutions established the fundamental rights of citizens for formerly enslaved people. Few people living in 1860 would have dared imagine either event, and yet, in retrospect, both seem to have been inevitable. In a beautifully crafted narrative, Edward L. Ayers restores the drama of the unexpected to the history of the Civil War. From the same vantage point occupied by his unforgettable characters, Ayers captures the strategic savvy of Lee and his local lieutenants, and the clear vision of equal rights animating black troops from Pennsylvania.","thumbnailUrl":"/book-covers/book-the-thin-light-of-freedom-the-civil-war-and-emancipation-in-the-heart-of-america-edward-l-ayers.jpg","readerProfile":{"readerLevel":"crossover","narrativeScore":0.027,"accessibilityScore":0.076,"scholarlyScore":0.196,"traits":[{"id":"popular","score":0.22,"confidence":"low","evidence":["trade publisher signal: Viking / Penguin Random House"]},{"id":"scholarly","score":0.2,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"historians\""]},{"id":"synthesis","score":0.2,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"from\"","catalog text contains \"history of\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":false,"statement":"","confidence":0},"academicOrientation":{"score":38,"confidence":0.4},"profileConfidence":0.72,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Pennsylvania","confidence":0.45}]},"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","p